Description:
 ros-jazzy-unitree-systems - ROS 2 control warped interface for Unitree Robots
Changes:
 ros-jazzy-unitree-systems (1.3.0-2026.09.03.14.06) noble; urgency=high
 .
   * Export motor temperatures as state interfaces
     LowState_ carries two temperatures per motor and both were discarded. A
     motor derates or faults on heat, so the only signal reaching the stack was
     the motorstate code that appears once the problem has already happened.
     Each joint now exports temperature and temperature2 next to position,
     velocity and effort. The values are degrees Celsius as sent, no scaling:
     measured against the robot they read 33-48 C at rest.
     Both are kept because they are not duplicates. They agree on 26 of the 29
     joints but differ on left_wrist_pitch, right_wrist_pitch and
     right_wrist_yaw, the differential wrists where two motors share a housing,
     so collapsing them would drop the hotter of a pair. HW_IF_TEMPERATURE covers
     the first; there is no standard name for a second. Neither is labelled motor
     or driver because nothing in the SDK or the documentation says which is
     which.
     No URDF change: this class overrides the legacy export_state_interfaces(),
     which the resource manager takes as given, so declaring these in the
     joint_interface macro would only push them into the MuJoCo block where
     MujocoSystem cannot source them.
     Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <mailto:noreply@anthropic.com>
     Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01DC9goz5FxQQqX19dntZS1w
   * Log through rclcpp and create the node before the DDS channels
     The three remaining std::cout calls bypassed the ROS logging system, so a
     CRC error on the DDS thread printed straight to stdout at the lowstate rate
     with no throttling, and none of it reached /rosout or the log files.
     They are now RCLCPP_WARN for a failed ReleaseMode, RCLCPP_ERROR_THROTTLE for
     a CRC error, and RCLCPP_INFO for the reported G1 type. Nothing returns ERROR:
     lowStateHandler returns void, and a sustained CRC storm already surfaces as a
     dead link because a rejected frame never reaches setData.
     Throttling the CRC message needs a clock, which exposed an ordering bug.
     lowstate_subscriber_->InitChannel() started delivering callbacks before
     node_ was created at the end of on_init, so lowStateHandler could run with
     node_ null. Harmless while it only used std::cout, a null dereference as
     soon as it used node_->get_clock(). The node and its joy publisher are now
     created before the DDS publisher and subscriber exist.
     Also corrects the lowstate rate in a comment: measured against the robot it
     is about 1 kHz, not the 500 Hz the comment claimed.
     Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
     Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01DC9goz5FxQQqX19dntZS1w
   * Report motor faults from read() and fail the hardware
     A non-zero motorstate was printed to stdout from the DDS callback and
     otherwise ignored, so a faulted leg motor left the stack running as if
     nothing had happened, on a robot that was no longer able to follow the
     commands it was being sent.
     The code is now carried in MotorState and checked in read(), which returns
     ERROR so ros2_control deactivates the controllers rather than continuing to
     drive a broken joint. Every faulted motor is reported before returning, so a
     multi-motor failure is visible at once, and the message names the joint
     rather than the motor index. RCLCPP_FATAL replaces std::cout; it needs no
     throttling because the ERROR takes the component out of ACTIVE and read() is
     not called again.
     All 29 motors are checked. The previous 0-11 bound came from the SDK's
     g1_ankle_swing_example, which only commands the legs, so the bound described
     that example's control scope rather than which motors can fault; the SDK's
     own h1_27dof_example checks every motor. A faulted arm or wrist means the
     robot cannot follow what it is being sent either.
     Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
     Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01DC9goz5FxQQqX19dntZS1w
   * Detect a dropped robot link and send commands from write()
     The ethernet link to the robot is physically unreliable. Nothing noticed
     when it dropped: motor_state_buffer_ kept handing read() the last state it
     ever received, so controllers ran on a frozen snapshot while the robot,
     receiving no lowcmd, timed out to damping and the joints moved away from
     where the stack believed they were. On reconnect the first packet the robot
     saw was a position target from before the drop, at full stiffness, against
     joints that had since drooped -- a violent step next to the operator.
     read() now returns ERROR once no valid LowState_ has arrived for
     stateTimeout. DataBuffer allocates a new MotorState per message, so a change
     of pointer identity is what marks the link live, timestamped with the time
     ros2_control already passes in. A CRC-invalid frame never reaches setData,
     so a stream of corrupt frames correctly reads as a dead link rather than as
     traffic. This also removes an unchecked dereference: getData() returns null
     until the first message and read() used to dereference it blind.
     Returning ERROR alone made things worse, which is why the command path moved
     too. Leaving ACTIVE stops write() being called, and write() was the only
     thing that ever refreshed motor_command_buffer_ -- so the last full-stiffness
     command froze there while the 2ms CreateRecurrentThreadEx thread, which knows
     nothing about the lifecycle, republished it to the robot forever.
     write() now builds the LowCmd_ and publishes it directly, and the thread and
     its buffer are gone. Not sending is the intended safe stop: the robot's own
     firmware times out to damping, which is what it already does during a cable
     drop. Publishing from write() also matches every maintained ros2_control
     driver; the recurrent-thread pattern came from unitree_sdk2's examples, which
     have no write() to be in.
     Three defects go with the thread: it was created detached and never joined,
     with ~RecurrentThread destroying the this-bound callable before ~Thread
     cancelled it, while members it touched every 2ms were already being
     destroyed; the command path no longer races the control thread; and a
     component that cannot reach ACTIVE without fresh state can no longer publish
     mode_machine 0, which the robot ignores.
     Measured cost of the publish on the control thread: 17us median, 27us p99,
     against a 2ms period.
     Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <mailto:noreply@anthropic.com>
     Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01DC9goz5FxQQqX19dntZS1w
   * Contributors: qiayuanl
